From: GapKeep <noreply@gapkeep.org>
Subject: usbmouse: a bounded report descriptor parser

GapKeep keeps the mouse's report protocol (the wheel, 0006), so the mouse
driver parses the HID report descriptor the device sends. The parser
(DecodeReport) trusted it: five usages overflowed a 4-entry array on the
stack, item data was read past the descriptor's end (and its u16 length
could wrap), a report size over 32 bits asserted, and the bit offsets
could wrap, so that ExtractUnsigned() read anywhere.

The parser and the field extraction move to usbmousereport.cpp, where
GapKeep's fuzzer (tools/fuzz_usbmouse.sh) compiles them on the host:

- every item's data is checked against the remaining length; long items
  are skipped by their size;
- at most 4 usages before an input item, report sizes up to 32 bits,
  collections nested up to 16 deep and closed only when open, report IDs
  1-255, reports up to 64 bytes and descriptors up to 4096;
- every field found must lie inside the report;
- fields are read bit by bit, only inside the report actually received;
- a report with another report ID than the mouse's is not the mouse's.

A malformed descriptor gets the mouse refused, with a log line. For a
well-formed one the parser finds what the original found. This replaces
the code patch 0001 fixed (the layout is still zeroed first). The
keyboard and mouse drivers also check that the HID class descriptor is
whole before they read it.

